No Polish law mentions hitchhiking, and on ordinary roads it is simply legal. The motorway ban is structural rather than named: [https://isap.sejm.gov.pl/isap.nsf/DocDetails.xsp?id=WDU19970980602 the ''Prawo o ruchu drogowym''] defines an ''autostrada'' (art. 2(3)) and a ''droga ekspresowa'' (art. 2(4)) as roads intended only for motor vehicles, and art. 45(1)(4) forbids using a road or any part of it contrary to its purpose — which is what keeps pedestrians off the carriageway, the shoulder and the slip roads. Art. 49(3) allows stopping or parking on those two road types only where it is marked, so a driver can legally pick you up at a ''Miejsce Obsługi Podróżnych'' (MOP) rest area and essentially nowhere else on them. Ask people directly at a MOP, or at the last petrol station before the motorway begins. On a ''droga krajowa'' or ''wojewódzka'' thumbing from the shoulder is fine.
